#########################################################################
# ==============================================================================
# SAY WHAT IS HAPPENING DURING THE SLOW PARTS
# ==============================================================================
# The automated scan spends most of its wall time in stages that used to print
# nothing at all. Measured on a full run:
#
#   Azure sweep, flying                                40 s
#   Azure mesh extraction + Keyence path clearance     11.9 min
#   Keyence raster, flying                            ~10 min
#   Keyence profiles -> mesh                           minutes
#   Eddy-current toolpath clearance gate               36 min
#
# With no output an operator cannot tell a working stage from a hung one, and
# will eventually kill a stage that would have finished. This is a reporting
# problem rather than a speed one: 36 min is an acceptable cost for a gate that
# stops the arm hitting the part, but only if the thing says so.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE OUTPUT LOOKS LIKE, AND WHY IT LOOKS LIKE THAT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PLAIN APPENDED LINES. No spinner, no carriage return, no ANSI, no in-place
# redraw. The cell runs the pipeline under `script`, and the resulting typescript
# is read afterwards as a file -- one that is already unreadable in places
# because something else in the stack emits escape sequences. Anything that
# looks good live and terrible in a log file is the wrong trade here: the log is
# how a run gets diagnosed the next day.
#
# EVERY LINE CARRIES A WALL CLOCK. A typescript has no timestamps of its own, so
# "when did this stage actually start" is otherwise unanswerable. It is also the
# quickest way for someone watching to see that the newest line is 4 s old and
# not 4 min old.
#
#   [14:22:01] keyence profiles -> mesh: working, 5.0 s elapsed
#   [14:26:31] keyence profiles -> mesh: working, 4 min 30 s elapsed
#   [14:27:12] keyence profiles -> mesh: done in 5 min 11 s
#
#   [14:31:07] clearance over 284514 waypoints: starting
#   [14:31:12] clearance over 284514 waypoints: 41200/284514 (14%) 5.0 s elapsed, ~29 s left
#   [14:31:42] clearance over 284514 waypoints: done, 284514 in 35.2 s (8083/s)
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR RULES
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. RATE LIMITED, and the fast path is a counter compare rather than a clock
#    read. A tight loop may call step() a million times, and reading the clock on
#    every one of them is real time spent deciding not to print. So step() only
#    increments a counter and compares it against a threshold, and the threshold
#    is re-aimed on each actual check so that checks land roughly every quarter
#    of the reporting interval however fast the loop turns out to be.
#
# 2. NO FAKE ETA. An estimate from the first few items of a loop whose cost is
#    uneven is worse than no estimate -- an operator who is told "4 s left" and
#    then waits 36 min stops believing the next one. An ETA appears only once the
#    block has run for `eta_after` seconds (2 s) AND has done at least 2% of the
#    work. Until then the line says elapsed and fraction only.
#
# 3. THE FINAL LINE ALWAYS PRINTS, including when the block exits by exception --
#    that is the case that matters most, because a stage that is killed or that
#    fails must still say how long it ran. A KeyboardInterrupt is reported as
#    "interrupted after ...", anything else as "FAILED after ...".
#
# 4. NESTING IS INDENTED, AND A FAST INNER BLOCK IS SILENT. Depth is tracked per
#    thread and each level indents two spaces. At depth 0 the opening and closing
#    lines always print. Deeper than that, the closing line prints only if the
#    block said something worth saying: it called .result() or .note(), it ran
#    longer than the reporting interval, or it raised. So a step() loop wrapped
#    around a per-item progress block does not produce one line per item, and a
#    slow or interesting inner block still speaks. This is the deliberate choice
#    between suppressing and indenting: BOTH, keyed on whether the inner block
#    had anything to say.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GETTING THE LINE TO THE OPERATOR FROM A WORKER THREAD
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# print() from a dgpy worker thread does not reliably reach the console: a bare
# thread has no dgpy context, and output from it is swallowed. Every long
# operation here is exactly that kind of thread when the pipeline is driven from
# a script rather than typed at the prompt, which is when progress reporting
# matters most.
#
# So progress_say() goes out the way the rest of this pipeline already does it
# (robot_supervisor.dpi's _say(), cable_envelope.dpi): through _capture_say() if
# a capture session has defined one -- it prints AND appends to the session log,
# so a swallowed print still leaves a record -- and through a plain flushed
# print() otherwise. progress_logfile() adds a file of its own for a run with no
# capture session, and progress_sink() lets a GUI or a test take a copy.
#
# The heartbeat thread calls InitFreeThread() before it says anything, for the
# same reason: a bare threading.Thread has no context. It touches nothing but
# builtins, so that is belt and braces rather than a requirement.

import os
import sys
import time
import threading
import datetime

# A .dpi is compiled as a FUNCTION BODY sharing one globals() dict with every
# other .dpi, so a top-level def or assignment is a LOCAL of the include unless
# it is declared global first. Everything another module touches is listed here.
global Progress
global progress
global progress_over
global progress_step
global progress_note
global progress_say
global progress_sink
global progress_logfile
global progress_depth
global PROGRESS_EVERY_S
global PROGRESS_ETA_AFTER_S
global PROGRESS_ETA_MIN_FRACTION
global PROGRESS_MAX_STRIDE
global PROGRESS_ENABLED
global PROGRESS_SINKS
global PROGRESS_LOG
global _progress_duration
global _progress_state
global _progress_lock

#: Seconds between progress lines. One line every 5 s is enough for an operator
#: to see that something is alive without burying the rest of the log: a 36 min
#: gate produces 432 lines, which is a page and a half of a typescript.
PROGRESS_EVERY_S = 5.0

#: How long a block must have run before it is allowed to estimate a finish
#: time. See rule 2 above.
PROGRESS_ETA_AFTER_S = 2.0

#: ...and how much of the work must be done. Both conditions, not either.
PROGRESS_ETA_MIN_FRACTION = 0.02

#: Ceiling on how many step() calls may go by between two clock reads. Bounds
#: the worst case where a loop starts fast and then slows down -- at the cap, a
#: loop that suddenly drops to 1 item/s would go quiet for a long time, so this
#: is a compromise rather than "as big as possible".
PROGRESS_MAX_STRIDE = 1 << 18

#: Global off switch. Nothing in the pipeline turns this off; it is here so a
#: test, or an operator who wants a clean transcript, can.
PROGRESS_ENABLED = True

#: Extra places every progress line is delivered, installed with progress_sink().
PROGRESS_SINKS = []

#: A file every progress line is appended to, or None. See progress_logfile().
PROGRESS_LOG = None

#: Per-thread nesting depth. A threading.local rather than a counter, because
#: two stages running on two threads must not indent each other.
_progress_state = threading.local()

#: Serialises line emission, so a heartbeat thread and its own stage's thread
#: cannot interleave half-written lines.
_progress_lock = threading.RLock()


def progress_depth():
    """How many progress blocks are open on THIS thread."""
    return getattr(_progress_state, "depth", 0)


def progress_sink(fn=None, remove=False):
    """Deliver every progress line to `fn` as well. Returns the sink list.

    For a GUI panel, a test, or anything that wants a copy. A sink that raises
    is dropped rather than being allowed to break the stage it is reporting on.
    """
    if fn is None:
        return PROGRESS_SINKS
    if remove:
        if fn in PROGRESS_SINKS:
            PROGRESS_SINKS.remove(fn)
    elif fn not in PROGRESS_SINKS:
        PROGRESS_SINKS.append(fn)
    return PROGRESS_SINKS


def progress_logfile(path=None):
    """Append every progress line to `path` as well, or None to stop.

    Worth setting for an unattended run: print() from a worker thread is not
    guaranteed to reach the console, and a file is. Returns the path in use.
    """
    global PROGRESS_LOG
    if path is not None:
        path = os.path.abspath(path)
        d = os.path.dirname(path)
        if d and not os.path.isdir(d):
            os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    PROGRESS_LOG = path
    return PROGRESS_LOG


def progress_say(text, depth=None):
    """One plain line, timestamped and indented, to everywhere it should go.

    Never raises. A progress line that takes down the stage it is reporting on
    would be a spectacularly bad trade.
    """
    if not PROGRESS_ENABLED:
        return
    if depth is None:
        depth = progress_depth()
    line = "  [%s] %s%s" % (datetime.datetime.now().strftime("%H:%M:%S"),
                            "  "*max(0, int(depth)), text)
    with _progress_lock:
        for sink in list(PROGRESS_SINKS):
            try:
                sink(line)
            except Exception:
                pass
        said = False
        try:
            # The pipeline's own path when a capture session is running: it
            # prints and it appends to the session log, so a print swallowed by
            # a context-less worker thread still leaves a record.
            say = globals().get("_capture_say")
            if say is not None:
                say(line)
                said = True
        except Exception:
            said = False
        if not said:
            try:
                print(line, flush=True)
            except Exception:
                pass
        if PROGRESS_LOG:
            try:
                with open(PROGRESS_LOG, "a") as f:
                    f.write(line + "\n")
            except Exception:
                pass


def progress_note(text):
    """A one-off line at the current nesting depth, through the same path.

    For the facts a stage learns as it goes -- "2968678 points in, 60367 after
    the crop" -- which belong next to the progress lines rather than in a
    separate print() that is indented differently and lost on a worker thread.
    """
    progress_say(text)


def _progress_duration(seconds):
    """Seconds as something an operator reads without counting digits."""
    s = float(seconds)
    if s < 0.0:
        s = 0.0
    if s < 10.0:
        return "%.1f s" % (s,)
    if s < 60.0:
        return "%.0f s" % (s,)
    if s < 3600.0:
        m = int(s//60)
        return "%d min %02d s" % (m, int(round(s - 60*m)) % 60)
    h = int(s//3600)
    m = int((s - 3600*h)//60)
    return "%d h %02d min" % (h, m)


class Progress(object):
    """A long operation that says how it is going. Use it as a context manager.

        with progress("clearance", total=len(poses)) as p:
            for pose in poses:
                ...
                p.step()

        with progress("keyence profiles -> mesh", heartbeat=True):
            call_the_blocking_service()

        with progress_step("cleaning the mesh") as s:
            rep = clean_mesh_for_planning(a, b)
            s.result("%d -> %d faces" % (rep["faces_in"], rep["faces_out"]))

    total     number of step()s expected, or None for "count them as they come".
    heartbeat run a background thread that reports elapsed time on its own. The
              only option for a blocking call this process does not drive --
              a ROS service, a subprocess -- which is most of the slow stages.
    every     seconds between lines (default PROGRESS_EVERY_S).
    unit      what is being counted, for the lines ("waypoints", "poses", ...).
    announce  print an opening line. Default: yes at depth 0, no when nested.
    eta_after seconds before an ETA may be shown (default PROGRESS_ETA_AFTER_S).
    quiet     say nothing at all; the block still times itself.
    """

    def __init__(self, what, total=None, every=None, unit="", heartbeat=False,
                 announce=None, eta_after=None, quiet=False):
        self.what = str(what)
        self.total = None if total is None else int(total)
        self.every = float(PROGRESS_EVERY_S if every is None else every)
        self.unit = unit
        self.heartbeat = bool(heartbeat)
        self.announce = announce
        self.eta_after = float(PROGRESS_ETA_AFTER_S if eta_after is None
                               else eta_after)
        self.quiet = bool(quiet)
        self.n = 0
        self.seconds = 0.0
        self._result = None
        self._spoke = False
        self._depth = 0
        self._t0 = None
        self._last_line = 0.0
        self._last_check = 0.0
        self._n_at_check = 0
        self._stride = 1
        self._next = 1
        self._stop = None
        self._thread = None

    # -- the fast path ---------------------------------------------------------
    #
    # Two bytecodes' worth of work in the common case: add, compare. Everything
    # expensive -- the clock, the formatting, the I/O -- is behind the compare,
    # and _check() re-aims the threshold so the compare stays true only about
    # four times per reporting interval however fast this loop turns out to run.
    def step(self, n=1):
        self.n += n
        if self.n >= self._next:
            self._check()

    def _check(self):
        now = time.monotonic()
        since = now - self._last_check
        done = self.n - self._n_at_check
        # Aim the next check a quarter of a reporting interval away, so a line
        # is never more than ~25% late, and clamp: 1 at the slow end (a loop of
        # one item per second should be checked every item, it costs nothing),
        # PROGRESS_MAX_STRIDE at the fast end.
        target = max(0.05, 0.25*self.every)
        if since > 1e-9 and done > 0:
            stride = int(done*target/since)
        else:
            stride = self._stride*4
        self._stride = max(1, min(int(stride), PROGRESS_MAX_STRIDE))
        self._next = self.n + self._stride
        self._n_at_check = self.n
        self._last_check = now
        if now - self._last_line >= self.every:
            self._emit(now)

    # -- what the caller can add ----------------------------------------------
    def note(self, text):
        """A line now, whatever the rate limit says. For facts, not for ticks."""
        self._spoke = True
        if not self.quiet:
            progress_say("%s: %s" % (self.what, text), self._depth)

    def result(self, text):
        """What this block achieved; goes on the closing line.

        Calling it is also what makes a NESTED block worth a closing line at
        all -- see rule 4 at the top of this file.
        """
        self._result = text
        self._spoke = True

    def set_total(self, total):
        """The total, once it is known. Safe mid-flight."""
        self.total = None if total is None else int(total)

    # -- lifecycle -------------------------------------------------------------
    def __enter__(self):
        self.start()
        return self

    def start(self):
        self._t0 = time.monotonic()
        self._last_line = self._t0
        self._last_check = self._t0
        self._depth = progress_depth()
        _progress_state.depth = self._depth + 1
        announce = self.announce
        if announce is None:
            announce = self._depth == 0
        if announce and not self.quiet:
            if self.total is not None:
                progress_say("%s: starting (%s)"
                             % (self.what, self._count(self.total)), self._depth)
            else:
                progress_say("%s: starting" % (self.what,), self._depth)
            self._spoke = True
        if self.heartbeat:
            self._start_heartbeat()
        return self

    def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc, tb):
        self.finish(exc_type)
        return False

    def finish(self, exc_type=None):
        if self._t0 is None:
            return
        self._stop_heartbeat()
        self.seconds = time.monotonic() - self._t0
        _progress_state.depth = self._depth
        # Rule 3: the closing line is not optional, and rule 4 decides whether a
        # NESTED block that had nothing to say is worth one.
        show = (self._depth == 0 or exc_type is not None or self._spoke
                or self.seconds >= self.every)
        if show and not self.quiet:
            progress_say(self.what + ": " + self._closing(exc_type), self._depth)
        self._t0 = None

    def _closing(self, exc_type):
        el = _progress_duration(self.seconds)
        if exc_type is KeyboardInterrupt:
            return "INTERRUPTED after %s%s" % (el, self._so_far())
        if exc_type is GeneratorExit:
            # progress_over() whose consumer broke out of the loop. Not a
            # failure and not worth shouting about, but still worth timing.
            return "stopped early after %s%s" % (el, self._so_far())
        if exc_type is not None:
            return "FAILED after %s (%s)%s" % (el, exc_type.__name__,
                                               self._so_far())
        if self.n:
            rate = self.n/self.seconds if self.seconds > 0 else 0.0
            done = "%s in %s%s" % (self._count(self.n), el,
                                   (" (%s/s)" % self._rate(rate))
                                   if rate > 0 else "")
        elif self._result:
            return "%s (%s)" % (self._result, el)
        else:
            return "done in %s" % (el,)
        return ("%s, %s" % (self._result, done)) if self._result else done

    def _so_far(self):
        if not self.n:
            return ""
        if self.total:
            return " (%s of %s done)" % (self._count(self.n),
                                         self._count(self.total))
        return " (%s done)" % (self._count(self.n),)

    def _count(self, n):
        return "%d%s" % (n, (" " + self.unit) if self.unit else "")

    def _rate(self, r):
        if r >= 100.0:
            return "%.0f" % (r,)
        if r >= 1.0:
            return "%.1f" % (r,)
        return "%.2f" % (r,)

    # -- the lines -------------------------------------------------------------
    def _emit(self, now=None):
        if self.quiet:
            return
        now = time.monotonic() if now is None else now
        self._last_line = now
        self._spoke = True
        elapsed = now - self._t0
        el = _progress_duration(elapsed)
        if self.total:
            frac = float(self.n)/self.total
            text = "%s/%s (%.0f%%) %s elapsed" % (
                self.n, self._count(self.total), 100.0*frac, el)
            # Rule 2: an ETA has to have earned the right to be believed.
            if (self.n > 0 and elapsed >= self.eta_after
                    and frac >= PROGRESS_ETA_MIN_FRACTION):
                left = elapsed*(self.total - self.n)/self.n
                text += ", ~%s left" % (_progress_duration(left),)
        elif self.n:
            text = "%s so far, %s elapsed" % (self._count(self.n), el)
        else:
            text = "working, %s elapsed" % (el,)
        progress_say("%s: %s" % (self.what, text), self._depth)

    # -- the heartbeat ---------------------------------------------------------
    def _start_heartbeat(self):
        self._stop = threading.Event()
        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self._beat,
                                        name="progress:%s" % (self.what[:32],))
        self._thread.daemon = True
        self._thread.start()

    def _beat(self):
        # A bare threading.Thread has no dgpy context, and output from one is
        # swallowed. This one touches nothing but builtins, but the context is
        # free and the failure mode without it is silence -- which is the exact
        # thing this file exists to prevent.
        try:
            from dataguzzler_python.context import InitFreeThread
            InitFreeThread()
        except Exception:
            pass
        poll = max(0.05, min(0.25, self.every))
        while not self._stop.wait(poll):
            # Never let this thread die on a bad line: a heartbeat that stops
            # beating looks exactly like the hang it is there to rule out.
            try:
                now = time.monotonic()
                if self._t0 is not None and now - self._last_line >= self.every:
                    with _progress_lock:
                        if self._t0 is not None:
                            self._emit(now)
            except Exception:
                pass

    def _stop_heartbeat(self):
        if self._stop is not None:
            self._stop.set()
        if self._thread is not None:
            self._thread.join(timeout=2.0)
        self._stop = None
        self._thread = None


def progress(what, total=None, **kwargs):
    """A progress block. See Progress for the arguments.

        with progress("clearance", total=n) as p:
            ...
            p.step()
    """
    return Progress(what, total=total, **kwargs)


def progress_step(what, **kwargs):
    """One timed operation, reported in a single closing line.

    No opening line and no ticking: this is for a step that is usually quick,
    might be minutes, and has a result worth stating afterwards. A heartbeat
    covers the "might be minutes" case, so a step that runs long stops being
    silent without a fast one costing a line.

        with progress_step("cropping") as s:
            ...
            s.result("2968678 -> 60367 points (98% discarded)")
    """
    kwargs.setdefault("heartbeat", True)
    kwargs.setdefault("announce", False)
    return Progress(what, total=None, **kwargs)


def progress_over(items, what, total=None, **kwargs):
    """Iterate `items`, reporting progress. Same arguments as Progress.

        for pose in progress_over(poses, "checking poses", unit="poses"):
            ...

    A sequence supplies its own total; a generator does not, so pass one if it
    is known -- without it the lines report a count and elapsed time, which is
    still the difference between "alive" and "hung".
    """
    if total is None:
        try:
            total = len(items)
        except TypeError:
            total = None
    with Progress(what, total=total, **kwargs) as p:
        for item in items:
            yield item
            p.step()
